17] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105900 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8a1ff93-7caf-5f2a-98fa-8f8ab77f9ec8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105901 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fa712994-dace-59ea-aaec-cc83ad4fb4a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1039 "Applications of LOTUS" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105902 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b181e0ec-2117-5c97-8b6a-dda79e5e1168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1045 "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105978 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "df06df55-18c1-571d-8ff5-d8bef7d927dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}-2X],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9},\\,0\\le x\\le 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3be545bd-521d-5693-98dc-347a06d189c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106031 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{3}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}-2X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2x)\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{9}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{54}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{18}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}]-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106910 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106910 #edb/ref 1061 453122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49816a73-0431-5661-9a70-b0f19f749956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106911 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106911 #edb/ref 1061 453123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a3e29fd-cad9-5476-bac7-05f605eb832a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106912 #edb/ref 1061 453124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d6ff61a-b40a-5779-8e0d-7e8ab94360bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106912 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106913 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106913 #edb/ref 1061 453127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106913 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd61e0e8-6780-536a-a264-b2f23461b6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106913 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106914 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106914 #edb/ref 1061 453128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106914 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7818225-2845-544f-834c-59feb1e8d177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106914 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106915 #edb/ref 1061 453131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e944ae9-96c9-5107-81b1-4d0a094457be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106916 #edb/ref 1061 453134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f442870-7bcf-5929-9ad4-d5ca7c6e4de1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107123 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f_X$ and support $S_X = \\{-1,0,1\\},$ shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nNow suppose that we define a new random variable $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nY = (X+1)^3.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ is quite a lot of work if we use only the methods we've seen up to now. Typically, we would need to find the probability mass function $f_Y$ of $Y,$ and then compute $\\textrm{E}[Y].$\n\nThankfully, there is a handy rule, called **the rule of the lazy statistician,** that allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ *without* having to find $f_Y$ first! This is useful because $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ might be the only quantity we're interested in. \n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X),$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] =  \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x).\n$$\n\nApplying the rule of the lazy statistician with the function $r(x) = (x+1)^3$ for our PMF $f_X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}r(x)\\,{f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}(x+1)^{3}\\,{f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =(-1+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.25+(0+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.5+(1+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & ={0}^{3}\\cdot 0.25+{1}^{3}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{3}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.25+1\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107123 #edb/ref 1065 5535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107123 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dba36f3b-5f7a-5e24-861a-b5635908f917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107123 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107124 #edb/ref 1064 "Although $\\textrm{E}[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm{E}[X])$ in general, equality is possible for some functions. \n\nFor example, we've already seen that if $a$ and $b$ are real constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[aX+b]=a \\, \\textrm{E}[X]+b.\n$$\n\nLet's prove this using the rule of the lazy statistician for a discrete random variable $X$. In this case, $r(X)=aX+b$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[aX+b] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}(ax+b)\\,f(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}ax\\,f(x)+{\\sum }_{x\\in S}b\\,f(x) \\\\  & =a{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\,f(x)+b{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) \\\\  & =a\\,\\text{E}[X]+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar proof can be conducted for continuous random variables. \n\nSimilarly, for functions $r(x)$ and $s(x)$ and real coefficients $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a \\cdot r(X) + b \\cdot s(X)] = a \\, \\textrm{E}[r(X)] + b \\, \\textrm{E}[s(X)].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107124 #edb/ref 1065 5560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107124 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41c8d9b6-bbfa-525a-bafd-ce7949043e4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107124 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Linearity of Expectation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107125 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ and let $Y=r(X)$. Then, the rule of the lazy statistician for continuous random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y]=\\textrm E[r(X)]=\\int^{\\infty}_{-\\infty} r(x)f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice the analogy with the discrete case." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107125 #edb/ref 1065 5563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107125 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9892132-3602-5dbf-a92b-6cfbab1207f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107125 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107134 #edb/ref 1064 "The rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X),$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] =  \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x).\n$$\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The reason for the name \"the rule of the lazy statistician\" is that the rule is sometimes mistaken as a definition when, in fact, it is a statement that requires rigorous proof (we won't do that here).\n\n- The rule is sometimes called the **law of the unconscious statistician** (or **LOTUS**).\n\n- In general $\\textrm E[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm E[X]).$\n\nThe rule has some nice intuition behind it. We demonstrate with a simple example.\n\nSuppose a spinner has four sides, labeled $1$ through $4.$ Let the random variable $X$ equal the result when the spinner is spun once, and let the function $Y = r(X)$ denote the profit made when the spinner lands on $X = x.$ To calculate the *expected* profit on each spin, we multiply each possible payout by the probability of that payout occurring and sum over all possible values of $X.$ \n\nLet's take a look at another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107134 #edb/ref 1065 6323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107134 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cfe6f3bd-33c7-5c0b-989d-7e0e0716bb9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107134 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Comments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437228 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186437255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437228 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186437256 #edb/r